1 VUPDATE
 Checks this system's VSI products, OpenVMS patches and security fixes
 against the VSI Service Platform (sp.vmssoftware.com), and downloads and
 installs the ones you choose. Like a package manager: VUPDATE CHECK
 lists what is new, VUPDATE UPGRADE installs it.

 Format

   VUPDATE action [parameters] [/qualifiers]

 The actions are LOGIN, LOGOUT, CHECK, SHOW, UPGRADE and HELP. A VSI
 portal account is needed (VUPDATE LOGIN once per node).

 VUPDATE never installs an OpenVMS upgrade: it reports one. Kits must pass
 VSI's signature check (PCSI SIGNED_KIT) or PRODUCT INSTALL refuses them.

2 LOGIN
 Asks for your VSI portal user name (e-mail) and password, checks them
 with the VSI sign-in service and saves them for later VUPDATE commands
 (including the nightly check) in VUPDATE$ROOT:[DATA]CREDENTIALS.DAT.

 The file is readable by its owner only. Its contents are sealed with a
 key derived from this node's identity, so a copy is useless on another
 node and a modified file is refused. This is not secrecy from privileged
 users: anyone who can read the file on this node can recover the
 password. Changing SCSNODE means logging in again.

 Format

   VUPDATE LOGIN [/USERNAME="e-mail"]

3 /USERNAME
   /USERNAME="e-mail"

 Gives the portal user name instead of being asked for it. Quote the
 value: in DCL an unquoted @ starts an indirect command file.

2 LOGOUT
 Deletes the saved credentials (all versions of the file).

 Format

   VUPDATE LOGOUT

2 CHECK
 Compares the installed products (PRODUCT SHOW PRODUCT) with the VSI
 catalogue for this architecture and OpenVMS version, and lists:

   OpenVMS upgrades   a newer OpenVMS kit (reported only)
   Patches            OpenVMS patch kits not yet installed, or newer ones
   Updates            newer kits of installed VSI layered products

 Each line shows the installed and the new version, and flags: "security"
 when the kit's description names a CVE or a security fix, "reboot" when
 the kit needs a restart, "field test" for a non-release kit.

 The catalogue is cached for an hour and package details for a day, in
 VUPDATE$ROOT:[CACHE]; a CHECK answered from the cache needs no network.

 Exit status: success (VUPDATE-S-CURRENT) when there is nothing to do,
 informational (VUPDATE-I-UPDATES) when there is, so a batch job can test
 $STATUS.

 Format

   VUPDATE CHECK [/qualifiers]

3 /ALL
 Also lists the products that are up to date and the installed VSI
 products the catalogue doesn't have.

3 /FIELD_TEST
 Also offers field-test, advance and early kits (versions starting A, B,
 E, T or X). Without it, such kits are offered only for a product whose
 installed version is itself a field-test one.

3 /OUTPUT
   /OUTPUT=file

 Writes the report to a file instead of the terminal.

3 /REFRESH
 Fetches the catalogue and package details now instead of using the
 cache.

3 /SECURITY
 Lists only the kits flagged as security fixes.

2 SHOW
 Shows the VSI portal's details of a product's newest kit for this node:
 version and release date, the OpenVMS versions it supports, whether it
 needs a restart, its files with sizes and MD5, the kits it needs first
 (marked when installed, or when made for another OpenVMS version), the
 other kits of the product and VSI's description.

 Format

   VUPDATE SHOW product [/FIELD_TEST] [/REFRESH]

 The product is the PCSI name (COBOL, OPENSSH) or a patch family
 (VMS842L2A_SYS).

2 UPGRADE
 Downloads and installs kits: the products named, or a whole class with
 /ALL, /PATCHES, /UPDATES or /SECURITY. Kits they need first are added
 when not installed already, and everything is put in install order.

 VUPDATE shows the list and asks once before installing. For each kit it
 then downloads the files, checks their MD5 against the portal's, unpacks
 the ZIP and runs PRODUCT INSTALL on your terminal (patches with
 /SAVE_RECOVERY_DATA, so PRODUCT UNDO PATCH can take them out again). The
 kit directory is deleted after a successful install and kept when one
 fails. VUPDATE stops after a kit that needs a reboot.

 Installing needs the privileges PRODUCT INSTALL needs.

 Format

   VUPDATE UPGRADE [product[,...]] [/qualifiers]

3 /ALL
 Every patch and update CHECK lists (not OpenVMS upgrades).

3 /CONFIRM
   /CONFIRM (default)
   /NOCONFIRM

 Asks before installing. /NOCONFIRM installs without asking (for batch
 jobs); PCSI may still ask kit-specific questions.

3 /NOCONFIRM
 Installs without asking first. See /CONFIRM.

3 /DOWNLOAD_ONLY
 Downloads, checks and unpacks the kits but doesn't install them. Each kit
 is left in its own directory under the work directory, ready for PRODUCT
 INSTALL.

3 /FIELD_TEST
 As for CHECK: also considers field-test kits.

3 /PATCHES
 Every OpenVMS patch CHECK lists.

3 /NOREBOOT
 Leaves out the kits that need a restart, and the kits that need one of
 those first. See /REBOOT.

3 /REBOOT
   /REBOOT (default)
   /NOREBOOT

 /NOREBOOT leaves out the kits that need a restart, and the kits that need
 one of those first. Use it to install what can go in without a reboot.

3 /REFRESH
 As for CHECK: fetches the catalogue and details now.

3 /SECURITY
 Every kit flagged as a security fix.

3 /UPDATES
 Every layered-product update CHECK lists.

3 /WORK
   /WORK=directory

 Where kits are downloaded and unpacked; default VUPDATE$ROOT:[KITS].
 Each kit gets a subdirectory.

2 HELP
 Shows this help. VUPDATE HELP UPGRADE /NOREBOOT goes straight to a topic.

 Format

   VUPDATE HELP [topic...]

2 Logical_names
 VUPDATE$ROOT          the installation root ([SYSEXE], [DATA], [CACHE],
                       [KITS], [HELP])
 VUPDATE$CREDENTIALS   another credential file than [DATA]CREDENTIALS.DAT
 VUPDATE$CA_FILE       another CA bundle than [DATA]CACERT.PEM
 VUPDATE$PROXY         a proxy URL for all VUPDATE connections

2 Examples
   $ VUPDATE LOGIN
   $ VUPDATE CHECK
   $ VUPDATE CHECK/SECURITY
   $ VUPDATE SHOW OPENSSH
   $ VUPDATE UPGRADE OPENSSH
   $ VUPDATE UPGRADE/PATCHES/NOREBOOT
   $ VUPDATE UPGRADE/ALL/DOWNLOAD_ONLY/WORK=DKA100:[KITS]
